---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="The Ground We Share"
  description="The common ground Pamoja stands on: the Dish With One Spoon Wampum, the Two Row Wampum, and the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms — Indigenous teachings we honour rather than own, and the law we all share."
  currentSection="ground"
>
  <section class="page-head wrap">
    <p class="tag">Soil · The Ground We Share</p>
    <h1>The ground we share.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A tree stands in soil it did not make. Pamoja's work stands on common
      ground held by everyone in this city: two Indigenous covenants that long
      predate us, and the Charter that belongs to all of us. Before anything
      about us, this page is about what we all stand on together.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!--
    TREATY CONTENT NOTICE: All wording on this page concerning the Dish With
    One Spoon Wampum and the Two Row Wampum is placeholder pending Abdo's
    final approval (brief §8: final approval on all treaty-related content).
    Per brief §9.1, no Haudenosaunee visual motifs are used anywhere: the
    covenants are represented only through words and abstract forms — one
    shared vessel, two parallel paths.
  -->

  <section class="wrap soil-grid" aria-label="The three elements of the common ground">
    <article class="ground-card">
      <figure class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 120 72" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round">
          <path d="M20 22 h80" />
          <path d="M26 22 c0 22 14 34 34 34 s34 -12 34 -34" />
          <path d="M52 64 h16" />
        </svg>
      </figure>
      <h2>Dish With One Spoon Wampum</h2>
      <p>
        The Dish With One Spoon is an Indigenous covenant of shared land: one
        dish, one spoon, no knife. Everyone eats from the same dish, so the
        dish belongs to everyone and no one may take so much that others go
        without. Around the Great Lakes it has governed how peoples share
        hunting grounds and resources for centuries.
      </p>
      <h3>What Pamoja has learned from it</h3>
      <p>
        Three principles we hold and practise:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Take only what you need.</li>
        <li>Leave some for others.</li>
        <li>Keep the dish clean.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These principles shape how Pamoja handles money, space, and credit —
        fair honorariums, no extraction, nothing taken that someone else needs.
      </p>
      <p class="honour-note">
        This is an Indigenous teaching. Pamoja honours it; we do not own it or
        claim it. We are learners and guests of this covenant.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="ground-card">
      <figure class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 120 72" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round">
          <path d="M8 26 c14 -10 28 -10 42 0 s28 10 42 0 c8 -6 16 -8 20 -6" />
          <path d="M8 48 c14 -10 28 -10 42 0 s28 10 42 0 c8 -6 16 -8 20 -6" />
        </svg>
      </figure>
      <h2>Two Row Wampum</h2>
      <p>
        The Two Row Wampum records a covenant between the Haudenosaunee and
        European newcomers: two vessels travelling the same river side by side
        — each carrying its own laws, customs, and ways — neither steering the
        other's path. An ethic of coexistence without erasure.
      </p>
      <h3>What Pamoja has learned from it</h3>
      <p>
        That difference does not have to be dissolved for people to travel
        together. Two paths, side by side, each whole: this is how Pamoja
        understands citizenship in a city of many origins, and it is the ethic
        our Two Row Citizens gatherings put into practice in Hamilton's parks.
      </p>
      <p>
        We learned this teaching from a friend and ally:
        <strong>Daniel Coleman, Professor Emeritus at McMaster University</strong>,
        who introduced us to the Two Row and walked with us as we took it up.
      </p>
      <p class="honour-note">
        This is an Indigenous teaching. Pamoja honours it; we do not own it or
        claim it. We are learners and guests of this covenant.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="ground-card">
      <figure class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 120 72" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round">
          <path d="M28 18 h64" />
          <path d="M28 30 h64" />
          <path d="M28 42 h64" />
          <path d="M28 54 h44" />
        </svg>
      </figure>
      <h2>Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms</h2>
      <p>
        The Charter is the written ground of shared life in Canada: fundamental
        freedoms of conscience, expression, assembly, and association, and the
        equality rights that protect every person in this country from
        discrimination. It is not a covenant from one people to another — it
        belongs to everyone who stands here.
      </p>
      <h3>What Pamoja has learned from it</h3>
      <p>
        That rights on paper become real in practice — in rooms, parks, and
        workplaces where people actually assemble, speak, worship, and gather
        as equals. Pamoja's gatherings put Charter freedoms to everyday use,
        and newcomer Canadians exercising them fully is part of what builds
        this city.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Charter sits in this soil alongside the covenants — the same
        weight, the same ground. Neither above nor below them.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap closing">
    <p class="closing-line">
      The soil is shared; the roots are ours. What Pamoja draws from this
      ground becomes <a href="/roots">our roots</a> — Ta'aruf and Takaful, the
      traditions of our own that we bring to the common ground.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-head {
    padding-block: 3rem 2rem;
  }
  .page-head h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.8rem, 7vw, 5.5rem);
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    max-width: var(--max-prose);
  }

  .soil-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(300px, 100%), 1fr));
    padding-block: 2rem 3rem;
    align-items: stretch;
  }
  .ground-card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    background: var(--white);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 14px;
    padding: 2rem 1.75rem;
  }
  .ground-card h2 {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }
  .ground-card h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--leaf-deep);
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0.5rem;
  }
  .ground-card p {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
  .ground-card ul {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }
  .ground-card li {
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }
  .glyph {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    color: var(--clay);
    max-width: 120px;
  }
  .honour-note {
    margin-top: auto;
    padding-top: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px dashed var(--line);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .closing {
    padding-block: 0 var(--space-section);
  }
  .closing-line {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    max-width: var(--max-prose);
  }
</style>
